{"agent_id":"session-115f7fc0-ee87-461e-af6a-37e547009c35","message":"Concrete strategy: make the dinosaur print-in-place, assembled on its belly with Z vertical; body, head/neck, torso, and all four legs are one fixed manifold (legs splayed slightly, broad flat undersides). Put three revolute joints only along the tail: fixed torso carries joint 1, carrying tail segment 1; segment 1 carries joint 2/segment 2; segment 2 carries joint 3/segment 3. Thus 3 Z-axis pins and 3 tail segments, with tail yaw in XY. Keep the pivot centers on the tail centerline and hide them inside overlapping tapered segment roots; taper the tail toward its tip. Size the complete posed bounding box around e.g. 90×42×48 mm, including pin heads and feet, staying below 100×50×60.

At each joint model a vertical stem attached to the upstream part, running through an annular receiver on the downstream part, with a captive enlarged head / keeper so the downstream piece cannot lift off or drop away. Use an internal relief chamber around the keeper and a narrower throat; give keeper and receiver throat/chamber 45° (or shallower) conical/chamfered transitions, not flat unsupported roofs. Leave the joint gap open to air, avoid horizontal bridges/ceilings and support-enclosed pockets; keep the pin stem, receiver walls, and ring walls vertical. Ensure all components have a bed-connected underside in the assembled print pose. Start with ~0.3–0.5 mm radial clearance and ~0.25–0.4 mm axial clearance (tune to printer/material), then verify by slicing at the intended layer height; use a short test joint first.

Pitfalls: distinguish 3 tail segments (so only 2 inter-segment boundaries) from 3 joints (include the body-to-first-tail pivot); do not accidentally add another joint at head/legs. Check the STL is a set of separate nonintersecting solids with clearance, not a fused mesh; OpenSCAD preview alone can hide coincident geometry, so render/export and inspect sliced layers. Too-small clearance fuses; too-large clearance weakens retention/wobble. Keep captive head larger than throat but fit cavity with clearance, and make all 45° ramps dimensionally viable for the chosen nozzle/layer height. Tail swing envelope may exceed size bounds, so size against the assembled static bounding box unless all articulation extrema must fit."}

